@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Troubleshooting & Debugging in Azure";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">Effective troubleshooting and debugging in Azure ensures application reliability, quick issue resolution, and optimal performance. Azure provides various tools for diagnosing and resolving issues efficiently.</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Common Issues in Azure Deployments</h2>
        <p>Applications deployed on Azure may encounter various issues related to configuration, performance, and networking. Some common issues include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Deployment Failures:</strong> Incorrect configurations, missing dependencies, or pipeline errors.</li>
            <li><strong>Application Crashes:</strong> Unhandled exceptions, memory leaks, or resource limits.</li>
            <li><strong>Performance Degradation:</strong> Slow response times due to inefficient queries or high resource utilization.</li>
            <li><strong>Networking Issues:</strong> DNS failures, firewall restrictions, or incorrect VNet configurations.</li>
            <li><strong>Authentication & Security Errors:</strong> Incorrect role assignments, expired tokens, or identity provider misconfigurations.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="text-center mt-4">
        <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/media/troubleshooting-azure.png" alt="Azure Troubleshooting Tools" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
        <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Azure Troubleshooting and Debugging Tools</small></p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Tools for Troubleshooting & Debugging in Azure</h2>
        <p>Azure provides various tools to help diagnose and resolve application issues:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Azure Monitor:</strong> Provides insights into application performance and logs.</li>
            <li><strong>Application Insights:</strong> Tracks application health, dependencies, and real-time telemetry.</li>
            <li><strong>Azure Log Analytics:</strong> Aggregates logs from multiple resources for centralized analysis.</li>
            <li><strong>Kudu Console:</strong> A built-in tool for Azure App Services to troubleshoot deployments.</li>
            <li><strong>Azure Network Watcher:</strong> Helps diagnose and resolve network connectivity issues.</li>
            <li><strong>Azure Security Center:</strong> Detects and mitigates security threats in the environment.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Step-by-Step Debugging Process</h2>
        <p>Follow these steps to effectively troubleshoot issues in Azure:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Review Deployment Logs:</strong> Check Azure DevOps pipeline logs for errors.</li>
            <li><strong>Analyze Application Logs:</strong> Use Application Insights to identify exceptions and failures.</li>
            <li><strong>Check Resource Utilization:</strong> Monitor CPU, memory, and disk usage in Azure Monitor.</li>
            <li><strong>Verify Networking Configurations:</strong> Use Network Watcher to diagnose connectivity issues.</li>
            <li><strong>Reproduce & Debug Locally:</strong> Test the issue in a local development environment.</li>
            <li><strong>Enable Diagnostic Logging:</strong> Capture detailed logs using Azure Diagnostics settings.</li>
            <li><strong>Automate Alerts:</strong> Set up alerts for performance degradation or security breaches.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Example YAML for Enabling Application Insights in CI/CD</h3>
        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded">
stages:
- stage: Deploy
  jobs:
  - job: DeployWithMonitoring
    steps:
    - task: AzureAppServiceSettings@1
      inputs:
        azureSubscription: 'MyAzureSubscription'
        appName: 'MyWebApp'
        appSettings: '[{"name":"APPINSIGHTS_INSTRUMENTATIONKEY", "value":"$(ApplicationInsightsKey)"}]'
        </pre>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Best Practices for Troubleshooting in Azure</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Enable Detailed Logging:</strong> Collect application, system, and security logs.</li>
            <li><strong>Use Live Metrics:</strong> Monitor application health in real time with Application Insights.</li>
            <li><strong>Implement Fault Tolerance:</strong> Use retries and circuit breakers to handle failures.</li>
            <li><strong>Automate Recovery:</strong> Set up auto-healing and scaling for high availability.</li>
            <li><strong>Regularly Review Alerts:</strong> Fine-tune alerting to avoid unnecessary notifications.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <p>By leveraging Azure’s troubleshooting tools, teams can quickly diagnose and resolve application issues, ensuring high availability and performance.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="d-flex justify-content-start mt-4"></div>
        <a href="@Url.Action("DeployAppsAzure", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">&larr; Previous: Deploying Applications on Azure</a>
    </div>
</div>
